://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What are warning signs of a possible food shortage?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Signals can include repeated local stockouts, poor harvest forecasts, disrupted transport, export restrictions, rapidly rising local prices and worsening household food-security assessments. No single signal proves a broad shortage."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I tell a food-shortage warning from a rumour?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Trace the claim to its original source, verify the date and location, read the measurement and forecast period, and look for independent evidence that measures the same issue."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does drought mean a famine will happen?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. Drought can damage crops and livelihoods, but outcomes also depend on exposure, markets, water, conflict, reserves, trade and assistance. Forecast risk should not be presented as a confirmed outcome."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which sources should I check for food-crisis warnings?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Use FAO crop and market updates, WFP reporting and IPC analyses where available. Local government and market information help determine how a broad warning applies to a particular area."}}]}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Should I stockpile food because of shortage rumours?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A viral rumour alone is not a reason to panic-buy. A modest rotating supply can help with ordinary disruptions, but it should fit your household, budget, space and local official guidance."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What foods are useful for an emergency pantry?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Choose familiar shelf-stable foods that match your diet and can be prepared safely with the water, power and